### Why your custom style draws nothing

When you switch from a provider’s pre-built style URL to **your own style**, the source definition is now yours to get right. The most common reason a custom style shows no layers (while the provider’s style works) is that the **`source-layer` names you’re using don’t match the tile source’s schema**.

### 1. Wire the source correctly  

**Prefer a TileJSON `url`** if the provider offers one:

```js
map.addSource('basemap', {
  type: 'vector',
  url: 'https://provider.example.com/tileset.json'   // TileJSON endpoint
});
```

*Why:*  
- The TileJSON supplies the tile URL template, `minzoom`/`maxzoom`, bounds, and—crucially—the list of **available `source-layer` names** (`vector_layers`).  
- If you hand-write a `tiles` array you must also supply those metadata fields yourself; otherwise MapLibre may request zoom levels that don’t exist or miss the layer list entirely.

If the provider only gives you a raw `{z}/{x}/{y}` template, use `tiles` and add the missing metadata:

```js
map.addSource('basemap', {
  type: 'vector',
  tiles: ['https://provider.example.com/tiles/{z}/{x}/{y}.pbf'],
  minzoom: 0,
  maxzoom: 14   // set to the range the provider actually serves
});
```

### 2. Verify the exact `source-layer` names  

Open the TileJSON (e.g. `https://provider.example.com/tileset.json`) and look at the `vector_layers` array. Each entry has a `id`—that is the exact string you must use in every style layer that reads from this source.

```json
{
  "vector_layers": [
    { "id": "transportation", "description": "..."},
    { "id": "water",          "description": "..."},
    { "id": "landuse",        "description": "..."}
    // …
  ]
}
```

**Common mistake:** using a name from the provider’s *style* (e.g. `road` or `road_label`) instead of the underlying tile schema name (`transportation`). The provider’s style may have renamed layers internally, but the raw tiles expose only the schema names.

**Fix:** Change every `source-layer` in your style to match the IDs from `vector_layers`.

```json
{
  "id": "my-roads",
  "type": "line",
  "source": "basemap",
  "source-layer": "transportation",   // <-- exact match
  "paint": { "line-color": "#ff0000", "line-width": 2 }
}
```

### 3. Insert your layers below the label symbols  

If you add layers with `map.addLayer()` without a second argument, they are placed **above** everything—including the provider’s label (`symbol`) layers. That can make it look like your data isn’t drawing because it’s simply covering the labels (or, conversely, being covered by a later raster layer).

**Typical pattern:**

```js
// Find the first symbol layer (labels) in the existing style
const firstSymbol = map.getStyle().layers.find(l => l.type === 'symbol').id;

// Add your custom layer *before* that symbol layer
map.addLayer(myLayerObject, firstSymbol);
```

### 4. Quick checklist

| Item | What to verify |
|------|----------------|
| **Source definition** | Use `url` → TileJSON if available; otherwise supply `tiles`, `minzoom`, `maxzoom`. |
| **`source-layer` names** | Exact strings from TileJSON’s `vector_layers`. |
| **Layer order** | Insert custom layers before the first `symbol` layer (or any raster layer that would obscure them). |
| **CORS** | Ensure the tile endpoint sends `Access-Control-Allow-Origin` (otherwise the map stays blank). |
| **Glyphs / sprites** | If you also need labels or icons, provide `glyphs` and `sprite` URLs; missing glyphs only change the font, missing sprites remove icons. |

### TL;DR fix

```js
// 1. Add the source (prefer TileJSON)
map.addSource('basemap', {
  type: 'vector',
  url: 'https://provider.example.com/tileset.json'   // <-- TileJSON endpoint
});

// 2. Use the exact source-layer names from that TileJSON
const myRoads = {
  id: 'my-roads',
  type: 'line',
  source: 'basemap',
  'source-layer': 'transportation',   // <-- matches TileJSON
  paint: { 'line-color': '#ff6600', 'line-width': 2 }
};

// 3. Insert before the first label layer
const firstLabel = map.getStyle().layers.find(l => l.type === 'symbol').id;
map.addLayer(myRoads, firstLabel);
```

After these steps your custom style should render on top of the same tile source that the provider’s style uses. If you still see nothing, double-check the network tab for CORS errors or 404s on the TileJSON URL.

Add the new layer **before** the first symbol (label) layer in the style.  
`map.addLayer()` can take a second argument – the id of the layer that should stay **above** the one you’re adding.  
If you pass the id of the first label layer, your data will be drawn underneath all of the street-name symbols.

```js
// 1️⃣ Find the first symbol layer (the one that draws labels)
let firstLabelId = null;
for (const layer of map.getStyle().layers) {
  if (layer.type === 'symbol') {
    firstLabelId = layer.id;
    break;   // stop at the first symbol layer
  }
}

// 2️⃣ Add your GeoJSON source (if you haven’t already)
map.addSource('my-data', {
  type: 'geojson',
  data: myGeojson
});

// 3️⃣ Add the layer *below* the labels
map.addLayer(
  {
    id: 'my-data-fill',
    type: 'fill',          // or line, circle, etc.
    source: 'my-data',
    paint: {
      'fill-color': '#ff6600',
      'fill-opacity': 0.4
    }
  },
  firstLabelId   // ← second argument: insert before this layer
);
```

### Why this works
* Layers are rendered **bottom-to-top** in the order they appear in the style.
* When you call `addLayer()` with no second argument, the layer is appended **above everything**, which hides the basemap’s label symbols.
* Supplying the id of the first `symbol` layer tells MapLibre to insert your layer right before that layer, preserving the label stack.

### Quick tip
If you know the exact label layer id (e.g., `"road-label"` in an OpenMapTiles style), you can pass that string directly instead of looping:

```js
map.addLayer(myLayerSpec, 'road-label');
```

But the loop shown above works for any style, even when the label layer names differ.

You’re almost there – the only thing you need to add for the standard OSM slippy-map tiles is the **`tileSize`** (and, optionally, some metadata such as attribution or zoom limits).  

```js
// 1️⃣ Add the raster source
map.addSource('osm-basemap', {
  type: 'raster',
  tiles: [
    'https://tile.openstreetmap.org/{z}/{x}/{y}.png'
  ],
  // OSM tiles are 256 px, while the raster source defaults to 512 px
  tileSize: 256,

  // optional but recommended
  minzoom: 0,
  maxzoom: 19,
  attribution: '© OpenStreetMap contributors'
});
```

```js
// 2️⃣ Add a raster layer that draws the source
map.addLayer({
  id: 'osm-basemap-layer',
  type: 'raster',
  source: 'osm-basemap',
  // Insert it below the first symbol layer so you don’t cover labels
  // (find the first symbol layer id programmatically if you want it robust)
  // e.g. map.addLayer(layerDef, firstSymbolLayerId);
});
```

### Why `tileSize: 256` matters
* The raster source defaults to `tileSize: 512`.  
* OSM’s tiles are 256 px, so without overriding the size the map will appear **zoomed out** (labels and other vector layers will be mis-aligned) and you’ll see a blurry or offset basemap, but no error is thrown.

### Optional extras (good practice)

| Property | Why you might want it |
|----------|-----------------------|
| `minzoom` / `maxzoom` | Constrains requests to the zoom range the server actually provides (OSM serves 0-19). |
| `attribution` | Required by OSM’s tile-use policy and shows up in the map’s attribution control. |
| `bounds` | Not required for OSM, but useful for custom tile sets. |
| `raster-fade-duration: 0` | If you don’t want the default fade-in effect when tiles load. |

### CORS
OpenStreetMap tiles already send the proper `Access-Control-Allow-Origin` header, so you don’t need any extra server configuration.

With the `tileSize: 256` (and the source-layer added to a raster layer) the OSM tiles will render correctly in MapLibre. Happy mapping!

No. A MapLibre **style** (the JSON that contains `version`, `sources`, `layers`, `sprite`, `glyphs`, etc.) is not a source that can be added with `map.addSource()`.  
A source is a single data provider (e.g., a vector-tile endpoint, a raster-tile endpoint, a GeoJSON object, …). The style JSON is a *collection* of sources **plus** the layer definitions that tell MapLibre how to draw from those sources.

To reuse just one of the background layers from that style you need to:

1. **Copy the source definition** that the background layer uses.  
   ```js
   // from the remote style JSON
   const bgSource = {
     type: 'vector',          // or 'raster' depending on the original
     url: 'https://example.com/tiles.json'   // prefer `url` when a TileJSON exists
     // if only a template is available, use `tiles: [...]` and add minzoom/maxzoom
   };
   map.addSource('bg-source', bgSource);
   ```

2. **Add the layer definition** (the background layer you want).  
   ```js
   const bgLayer = {
     id: 'my-bg',
     type: 'background',          // or 'fill', 'raster', etc. as in the original
     source: 'bg-source',
     // for vector sources you must also set `source-layer` to the exact name
     // that appears in the TileJSON `vector_layers` list
     'source-layer': 'background',   // <-- match exactly
     paint: {
       'background-color': '#f0f0f0'
     }
   };
   // Insert it below the first symbol layer so it doesn’t cover labels
   const firstSymbol = map.getStyle().layers.find(l => l.type === 'symbol')?.id;
   map.addLayer(bgLayer, firstSymbol);
   ```

3. **Provide the style-root assets** that the original style used, if the layer needs them.  
   * A background layer usually does not need `glyphs` or `sprite`.  
   * If you later add any symbol layers from the same style, you must also set `map.setStyle({glyphs: ..., sprite: ...})` or copy those URLs into your own style definition.

4. **Check the usual wiring points** (the things that silently break a layer):  

   * **`source-layer` name** – must match the tile schema exactly; look at the TileJSON’s `vector_layers` field.  
   * **Zoom range** – if you used a hand-wired `tiles` array, be sure to set `minzoom`/`maxzoom` so MapLibre doesn’t request zoom levels the tileset doesn’t have.  
   * **CORS** – the tile endpoint must send `Access-Control-Allow-Origin` headers; otherwise the requests are blocked and the map stays blank.  
   * **Layer order** – add the background layer *before* the first `symbol` layer (or any other layer you want to stay on top) to avoid covering labels.

That’s all you need. You cannot treat the whole style JSON as a source; you must extract the needed source definition, add it, then add the specific layer(s) you want to reuse, making sure the `source-layer` names and other metadata line up with the tile data.

`setFeatureState` works only on features that have an **id** that MapLibre can see.  
Your parcels are coming from a **GeoJSON** source, so the features only have the
`parcel_id` property inside `properties`. Unless you tell the source to promote
that property to the feature’s id, MapLibre treats every feature as *id-less*,
and `setFeatureState` silently does nothing (the expression never matches and
`getFeatureState` returns `{}`).

### Fix – promote the `parcel_id` property

Add `promoteId: 'parcel_id'` (or an object if you have multiple source-layers)
when you create the source:

```js
map.addSource('parcels', {
  type: 'geojson',
  data: parcelsGeojson,   // your FeatureCollection
  promoteId: 'parcel_id' // <-- tells MapLibre to use this property as the id
});
```

Now each feature’s id is the value of its `parcel_id` property, and you can
address it with `setFeatureState`:

```js
// Example: highlight on mouse move
map.on('mousemove', 'parcel-fill', (e) => {
  // clear previous hover state
  if (hoveredId !== null) {
    map.setFeatureState(
      { source: 'parcels', id: hoveredId },
      { hover: false }
    );
  }

  // set new hover state
  const feature = e.features[0];
  hoveredId = feature.id;               // <-- id comes from promoteId
  map.setFeatureState(
    { source: 'parcels', id: hoveredId },
    { hover: true }
  );
});
```

### Paint expression

Your layer’s paint must read the state, e.g.:

```json
{
  "id": "parcel-fill",
  "type": "fill",
  "source": "parcels",
  "paint": {
    "fill-color": [
      "case",
      ["boolean", ["feature-state", "hover"], false],
      "#ffcc00",          // color when hovered
      "#888888"           // default color
    ],
    "fill-opacity": 0.6
  }
}
```

### Common gotchas to double-check

| Issue | What to verify |
|-------|----------------|
| **`promoteId` missing** | Add it as shown above. |
| **`parcel_id` not unique** | Each feature must have a unique value; otherwise state will be shared. |
| **Source not yet loaded** | Call `setFeatureState` only after the `data` event (`map.on('data', …)` with `e.sourceDataType === 'source' && e.sourceId === 'parcels'`). |
| **Wrong source/layer name** | Ensure the `source` in `setFeatureState` matches the source id (`'parcels'`). |
| **Using a vector source** | For vector tiles you would also need `promoteId` (object form if layers differ). |
| **Feature id is a string** | Strings are fine; just be sure the value you pass to `setFeatureState` matches exactly. |

Once `promoteId` is set, `map.setFeatureState({source:'parcels', id:'12345'}, {hover:true})`
will correctly attach the state, the paint expression will react, and
`map.getFeatureState({source:'parcels', id:'12345'})` will return the stored
state.
